A length-one atomic vector is a single value, not an array, unless it is
wrapped in \code{\link[=I]{I()}} or \code{auto_unbox = FALSE}. A matrix is a flat array in
column-major order. A vector whose class zucbor does not know is written
as its underlying type.

Whole doubles become integers because R has no integer literal: \code{-7}
written in R is a double, and as a CBOR float it would be a different
value from the integer a COSE algorithm identifier needs. So \code{1L} and \code{1}
encode identically.

These have no CBOR form and raise \code{zucbor_unsupported_type}: complex
numbers, functions, environments, external pointers, S4 objects,
\code{POSIXlt} (convert with \code{\link[=as.POSIXct]{as.POSIXct()}}) and data frames. Names that are
partly missing, \code{NA} or empty are \code{zucbor_invalid_argument}, and two keys
that encode identically are \code{zucbor_duplicate_key}.
